B.H5=new A.ap("Energy expenditure by amputation level (traumatic vs vascular)?","Traumatic TT: 25% above normal. Vascular TT: 40% above normal. Traumatic TF: 60-70% above normal. Vascular TF: 100% above normal (double!). Higher level = more energy. Vascular etiology = more energy than traumatic at same level.")
B.GX=new A.ap("Most common contracture patterns: TT vs TF?","Transtibial: knee FLEXION contracture (hamstrings pull tibia into flexion, patients rest with knee bent). Transfemoral: hip FLEXION, ABDUCTION, and EXTERNAL ROTATION (unopposed hip flexors, abductors, external rotators). Prevention: prone positioning, stretching, no pillows under knee.")
B.H0=new A.ap("What is an IPOP (immediate postoperative prosthesis)?","Rigid dressing with pylon and prosthetic foot applied in the operating room immediately after amputation. Benefits: edema control, wound protection, limb shaping, early weight-bearing, psychological benefit. Leads to faster prosthetic fitting and higher ambulation success rates.")
B.HR=new A.ap("Residual limb wrapping: technique and purpose?","Figure-of-eight pattern with elastic bandage. Purpose: shape the limb into a conical/cylindrical form for socket fitting and control edema. Apply MORE pressure distally than proximally (distal-to-proximal gradient). Proximal constriction = tourniquet effect = bulbous distal limb.")
B.Hm=new A.ap("Leading cause of lower extremity amputation in the US?","Peripheral vascular disease (PVD) and diabetes: approximately 82% of all LE amputations. Trauma: ~16%. Cancer: ~1%. Congenital: ~1%. Dysvascular incidence increases with age, poorly controlled diabetes, and smoking.")
B.Hl=new A.ap("Phantom limb pain vs phantom limb sensation?","Phantom sensation: non-painful awareness of the missing limb (tingling, pressure, warmth). Very common (80-100% of amputees). Phantom pain: painful sensations in the missing limb (burning, cramping, shooting). Occurs in 50-80% of amputees. Both are neurological, not psychological.")
B.Hb=new A.ap("Targeted Muscle Reinnervation (TMR): what is it?","Surgical transfer of residual amputated limb nerves to alternative muscle sites. When patient thinks about moving missing limb, reinnervated muscles contract producing EMG signals for myoelectric control. Also reduces phantom pain and neuroma pain. Enables intuitive multi-function prosthetic control.")
B.HF=new A.ap("Osseointegration in prosthetics: what is it?","Titanium fixture surgically implanted into the medullary canal of residual bone. Prosthesis attaches directly to skeleton without a socket. Eliminates socket problems (skin breakdown, heat, pistoning). Provides osseoperception. Risk: infection at percutaneous site. Requires good bone quality.")
B.HP=new A.ap("Pediatric prosthetic milestones: UE and LE?","UE: passive mitt at 6 months (sitting). Active TD at 15-18 months. Myoelectric at 2.5-3 years. LE: first prosthesis at 9-12 months (pulling to stand). Simple SACH foot, no articulating knee initially.")
B.H4=new A.ap("Terminal overgrowth in pediatric amputees: what is it?","Appositional bone growth at the transected bone end creating a sharp spur that can penetrate soft tissue and skin. NOT from growth plates. Most common in: humerus > fibula > tibia > femur. Often requires surgical revision (stump capping).")
B.GD=new A.ap("Bilateral TF amputee: prosthetic height guidelines?","Set prostheses 2-4 inches SHORTER than pre-amputation height. Lowers center of gravity for better balance and stability. Reduces energy expenditure. Stubby prostheses (short, no knee joints) may be used initially during training.")
B.H2=new A.ap("Diabetic patient prosthetic considerations?","Priority: pressure distribution and skin protection (neuropathy means they cannot feel breakdown). Total contact sockets, gel liners essential. Regular skin monitoring. Contralateral limb also at risk. Frequent follow-up. Volume changes common due to fluid shifts.")
B.Ha=new A.ap("What is the difference between residual limb maturation phases?","Acute (0-6 weeks): wound healing, edema reduction, desensitization. Preprosthetic (6 weeks-6 months): shaping, strengthening, ROM, preparatory prosthesis. Definitive (6+ months): stable volume, definitive prosthesis fabrication. Volume typically stabilizes by 12-18 months.")
B.HJ=new A.ap("What are the components of a transfemoral prosthesis from proximal to distal?","Socket (quad or IRC) -> suspension system -> knee unit -> pylon (shank) -> prosthetic foot -> cosmetic cover (optional). The knee unit and foot type are selected based on K-level, activity needs, and patient goals.")
B.GW=new A.ap("What is pattern recognition in myoelectric control?","Machine learning algorithms analyze EMG patterns from multiple electrodes. Recognizes distinct muscle activation patterns for different movements (hand open, wrist rotation, grip types). Allows more intuitive, simultaneous multi-DOF control vs traditional two-site on/off control.")
B.HM=new A.ap("What are the contraindications for immediate prosthetic fitting?","Severe peripheral vascular disease with non-healing wound, active infection, cognitive impairment preventing safe use, unstable medical condition, open/contaminated wound, and severe bilateral amputation without adequate support system for training.")
B.GF=new A.ap("Desensitization techniques for residual limb?","Progressive exposure to textures: soft cloth -> rough towel -> massage -> tapping -> vibration. Also: weight-bearing on the limb, compression with shrinker or wrapping, mirror therapy for phantom pain. Goal: normalize sensation and reduce hypersensitivity for socket tolerance.")
B.GV=new A.ap("What is the Chopart amputation challenge and how is it managed?","Chopart (midtarsal disarticulation) tends toward equinovarus deformity from unopposed Achilles pull after loss of anterior tibialis and peroneal insertions. Managed with: Achilles tendon lengthening at surgery, rigid AFO or clamshell prosthesis, careful muscle balancing.")
B.HO=new A.ap("Myelomeningocele L3 level: what orthosis is needed?","Bilateral KAFOs. L3 preserves hip flexion/adduction and partial quads, but loses knee flexion, ankle dorsiflexion, and plantarflexion. Higher lesions (T12-L2) need HKAFOs. Lower lesions (L5-S1) need only AFOs. Lesion level determines ambulatory potential.")
B.H1=new A.ap("How often do pediatric amputees typically need prosthetic revisions?","Socket changes every 6-12 months during rapid growth. Full prosthesis replacement every 12-18 months. Growth is the primary reason. Adjustable sockets and modular components can extend time between replacements. Growth spurts may require more frequent changes.")
B.Mb=s([B.H5,B.GX,B.H0,B.HR,B.Hm,B.Hl,B.Hb,B.HF,B.HP,B.H4,B.GD,B.H2,B.Ha,B.HJ,B.GW,B.HM,B.GF,B.GV,B.HO,B.H1],t.x7)
